Certainly! An in-memory cache using a JavaScript object is straightforward and can be quite effective for a single-instance service with a limited request footprint. Here's a basic example using Express:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

const cache = {}; // our in-memory cache

app.get('/data/:id', (req, res) => {
  const cacheKey = req.originalUrl;
  if (cache[cacheKey]) {
    console.log('Cache hit:', cacheKey);
    return res.json(cache[cacheKey]);
  }

  // Simulate fetching data
  const data = { id: req.params.id, value: Math.random() };

  cache[cacheKey] = data; // store response in cache
  console.log('Cache miss:', cacheKey);
  res.json(data);
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on port 3000'));
```

This caches responses keyed by the full request URL, which works well for GET requests with query parameters. Things to consider:
- This cache never expires, so memory usage will grow indefinitely.
- It doesn't share state across multiple server instances.
- You might want to handle POST/PUT differently, as they generally shouldn't be cached.

