Absolutely, an in-memory JavaScript object can be a simple and effective starting point for caching in a Node.js REST API. Here's a basic example of how you might set it up:

```javascript
const cache = {};

function cacheMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  const cacheKey = req.originalUrl;
  if (cache[cacheKey]) {
    console.log('Serving from cache:', cacheKey);
    return res.json(cache[cacheKey]);
  }

  // Override res.json to capture the response body
  const originalJson = res.json.bind(res);
  res.json = (body) => {
    cache[cacheKey] = body;  // Store in cache
    originalJson(body);
  };

  next();
}
```

You would use this `cacheMiddleware` in your route handlers, and it will return cached responses when available. Some notes: this cache will be lost when your server restarts, it doesn't handle cache expiry or size limits, and it caches responses solely based on the request URL, ignoring headers or body. Depending on your needs, those might be important enhancements! Let me know if you'd like help adding any of them.

Absolutely, an in-memory cache can be a great way to speed up your API for repeated requests. Here's a straightforward example of how you might implement this in your Node.js REST API:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const port = 3000;

// Simple in-memory cache
const cache = {};

// Middleware to check cache before processing
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const key = req.originalUrl;
  if (cache[key]) {
    console.log('Serving from cache:', key);
    return res.json(cache[key]);
  }
  // Override res.json to cache the response before sending
  const originalJson = res.json.bind(res);
  res.json = (body) => {
    cache[key] = body;
    originalJson(body);
  };
  next();
});

app.get('/data', (req, res) => {
  // Simulate expensive operation
  setTimeout(() => {
    res.json({ message: 'This is some data', timestamp: Date.now() });
  }, 1000);
});

app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Example app listening at http://localhost:${port}`);
});
```

This middleware looks for a cached response keyed by the request's original URL. If it finds one, it immediately returns it. If not, it lets the request proceed, then caches whatever JSON response gets sent.

A few things to be aware of:

- This cache is **per server instance** and will be empty when your server restarts.
- It doesn't implement any eviction policy, so it will grow indefinitely if your API handles many unique URLs.
- This caches only JSON responses returned through `res.json`.

Depending on your needs, you might want to add TTL (time-to-live) expiration or switch to a more robust caching layer like Redis for shared caching across multiple instances or persistent caching. But for lightweight scenarios, this is a nice simple start!

Absolutely, an in-memory cache using a plain JavaScript object can be a simple and effective way to speed up your API responses. Here's a pattern I often recommend, which balances simplicity with some basic cache management:

```js
const cache = {};
const CACHE_TTL = 5 * 60 * 1000; // 5 minutes

function cacheResponse(key, data) {
  cache[key] = {
    data,
    expiresAt: Date.now() + CACHE_TTL
  };
}

function getCachedResponse(key) {
  const entry = cache[key];
  if (!entry) return null;
  if (Date.now() > entry.expiresAt) {
    delete cache[key];
    return null;
  }
  return entry.data;
}
```

You can use `cacheResponse` inside your route handlers to save results, and `getCachedResponse` to check before doing expensive work.

