Absolutely, an in-memory cache using a plain JavaScript object is straightforward to implement and can give you a nice speed boost for frequently requested resources. Here's a simple example of how you might do this in your Express.js app:

```js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

const cache = {};

app.get('/some-endpoint', (req, res) => {
  const cacheKey = req.originalUrl;
  if (cache[cacheKey]) {
    console.log('Serving from cache');
    return res.json(cache[cacheKey]);
  }

  // Simulate expensive computation or DB call
  const data = { message: 'Hello, world!', time: Date.now() };

  // Store response in cache
  cache[cacheKey] = data;

  res.json(data);
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on port 3000'));
```

This example uses the full request URL as the cache key, which works well for GET requests where the URL uniquely identifies the resource. When a request comes in, your app checks if the response is already cached — if so, it immediately returns the cached data; if not, it computes the response, caches it, and then returns it.

A few things to consider:
- This simplest form of caching never expires entries; you'll probably want to implement TTL (time-to-live) logic.
- The cache lives only as long as your server process does, so it won't survive restarts.
- If you run multiple server instances, each will have its own cache (which may or may not be what you want).

