You don't need a try/catch around *every* single async call if your overall control flow is structured to handle errors centrally. For example, in an Express app, it's common to have a single error-handling middleware that catches any errors passed via `next(err)`. To make this work smoothly with async/await, many people use a small helper that wraps async route handlers and forwards errors automatically, so you don't have to litter try/catch everywhere:

```js
const asyncHandler = fn => (req, res, next) => {
  Promise.resolve(fn(req, res, next)).catch(next);
};

// Usage:
app.get('/users', asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {
  const users = await getUsers();
  res.json(users);
}));
```

This pattern safely embraces async/await syntax while ensuring errors don't slip through the cracks. So yes, you can (and should) use async/await throughout your codebase—just make sure you have a centralized error-handling strategy, and use a pattern like this to bridge async errors into your framework's error middleware. The crashes from unhandled rejections usually happen because *no one* was catching the errors, not because async/await was used.

You're right that async/await is the modern best practice for writing clean, readable asynchronous code in Node.js — it's how most people write async code these days, and for good reason. But the concern about unhandled promise rejections is valid: an unhandled rejection can indeed crash your process in recent Node versions (since the behavior changed to be more strict), so you do need to handle errors *somewhere*.

That said, you absolutely don't need to sprinkle `try/catch` around *every* async function call. The best pattern I've seen — and use myself — is to have a centralized error handling middleware in your Express (or similar) app that catches rejected promises from your route handlers. For example, you can write a small helper like this:

```js
const asyncHandler = fn => (req, res, next) => {
  Promise.resolve(fn(req, res, next)).catch(next);
};
```

Then wrap your routes:

```js
